> Having met Eric and seen his writings for some time, I've come to
> respect him quite a bit. I think he's frightfully clever. However,
> while I understand and appreciate the point he's making here, I'd have
> to regard it at least a little bit as a kind of sophistry.

Only in that I couldn't make the argument in sufficient detail and with
all terms properly defined at the length I felt constrained to. (I was
on my way out the door for a weekend of live-action gaming).

By the way, since you've said that you "respect me quite a bit", let me
remind you that I attribute a substantial part of my intelligence advantage
to the fact that I've internalized GS principles.  Thus, you must conclude
either that (a) I am lying, (b) GS principles really *do* increase one's
effective intelligence, or (c) that I am *so* freakin' brilliant that
I can impress *you* as "frightfully clever" even though I've internalized
a bunch of stupidifying hooey.

Now.  Which one of those hypotheses sounds most plausible to you? :-)
 
> First of all, it is certainly true that two-value logic, while useful
> for lots of different kinds of analyses, isn't really the way the
> human brain works, let alone how the universe works. A common computer
> science exercise is the creation of "neural nets", essentially great
> taxonomies: "Is it a chair? Is it a dinosaur?" But taxonomies are
> fundmentally flawed because individual objects don't have intrinsic
> categories any more than goods have intrinsic value. The "category"
> of a thing depends on what you're doing with it. I call this the "Yew
> Tree" principle. The yew tree is a deciduous conifer. (Which breaks
> the typical notion of conifers as evergreens.)

OK, this is good GS-style analysis so far.
 
> However, notice how I started my previous paragraph and look at Eric's
> statement above: "... is generally false." Both he and I inevitably
> resorted to true-false statements to describe what we think.

Damn, I was afraid this would happen.

I was writing very quickly and trying to avoid more GS jargon than the bare
minimum.  What I *stopped* myself from writing was "generally false-to-fact".

The term "false-to-fact" is used in GS to mark the difference between false-
hood in some implied formal system (what mathematicians and logicians study)
and falseness in the observed universe (a false statement is one which
makes incorrect predictions about observation).

GS teaches that this distinction is fundamental.  When I wrote "Aristotelian
logic is generally false", what I was actually *thinking* is "Aristotelian
logic is false-to-fact (that is, it leads to poor predictions)."

It is not surprising that you would think I was implicitly referring to some
kind of two-valued formal-system "false".  One of the most common and
dangerous of the bad semantic habits GS was invented to fight is the habit
of confusing formal-systems "truth" with predictive "truth".

This bad habit is endemic in the non-GS-trained.  Also, I happen to know
that you are an Objectivist.  Objectivism's epistemology reinforces this
bad habit --- in fact, Rand's version is is actually *founded* on this
particular map-territory error (though David Kelley's version may not be;
I haven't read his stuff yet).  I have explained this in detail in
previous postings.

The fact is that I do *not* "inevitably resort to true-false statements"
to describe what I think, not in the sense you mean.  The fact that I
do not is one of the consequences of having internalized GS principles,
and I sincerely believe it is one of the things that *makes* me "frightfully
clever" even by your standards.

> While I would agree that it is a mental error to apply two-value logic
> in cases that allow for a wider interpretation of the facts, I don't
> necessarily consider this a refutation of the non-contradiction axiom.
> Rather, I consider it a reminder that where the non-contradiction axiom
> seems to be violated, I must have missed some of the facts of the
> situation. (e.g. that an object can be both "hot" and "cold" depending
> on what one compares it to.) Additionally, it's probably worthwhile
> to note that the non-contradiction axiom seems to be violated more
> often than not when one is dealing loosely with the facts.

Right stick, wrong end.  It is positively bloody amazing how close you
came to getting the fundamental GS insight here and still landed backwards.

The lesson of GS is that, in any domain of discourse outside of a formal
mathematical system, *you have always missed some of the facts*.  That
is not a contingent, avoidable problem; it is inherent in the process of
observation and abstraction from reality into language, *and it is why
Aristotelian logic is insufficient*!

To put it another way: you can deal with formal systems "tightly" in your
sense (thus the axioms of non-contradiction and excluded middle are among
the valid formal postulates for mathematics).  You cannot in general deal
with *facts* "tightly", because there are inevitably gaps and slop in
the predicates you use to represent them.  The map is not the territory.
 
> Probably, Eric doesn't intend to "do away with" the non-contradiction
> axiom, but I get the feeling that at least some people are hearing it
> that way. I know I did when I first heard about the yew tree.

And at least one list member has obviously made exactly this mistake, thus his
haring off on Intuitionism, etc.  I'm going to refer him to this reply.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

