Date: Mon, 19 Apr 93 11:31:19 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Singularity Studies

I make my assumptions on the basis that a simple hypothesis that
explains the observations is the first one to try.  We observe that
historically (over the whole of human history since the introduction
of agriculture) population has followed a hyperbolic trend.

The nature of such a trend means there is something that goes
up with population that affects the rate of population growth.
The simplest hypothesis I can make is that there is some property
that produces population growth that is constant *per person*.
I make a hypothesis that this property is inventiveness, ie.e
that the distribution of inventiveness is constant.  I am
implying the proportion of inventors in any given population is
about the same, and has remained the same over history.

Your comment contradicts the definition of 'progress' I made.
I explicitly said "I assume that 'progress' is measured by the
growth of human population".  Since I am using the tern in
a non-standard way, I put quotation marks around it.  By my
definition, the countries progressing the most are those that
have the highest population growth rates.  They rely on inventions
provided by the more developed parts of the world to create
the imbalance between birth and death rates.  But I never
claimed my hypothesis applied to individual countries, or to
the last few decades.  It applies to the human population as
a whole, and to the whole span of human history.  Also, until
the last few decades, the US was an example of a country with
high poulation growth rates (4% per year) and high technological
growth.  I don't see any correlation on a nation-scale of
population growth rate and technology.

Dani Eder

